# Served by Workers Assets; this file is not itself published.
#
# `noindex` tells a crawler not to list this site in search results. It is a *second*
# signal: robots.txt already disallows crawling entirely, and a crawler that obeys that
# never fetches a page and so never sees this header. The two cover different bots —
# robots.txt stops the compliant ones from reading anything, this stops anything that
# crawls anyway from publishing it.
#
# The gap neither closes: a search engine can still list a URL it only learned about from
# a link elsewhere, because it has read neither file. Closing that means allowing the
# crawl so the directive below can actually be read. See
# .scratch/content-pages/02-legal-notice-private-site.md.
#
# Link previews are a separate question from indexing, and robots.txt is what decides
# them: the unfurl bots listed there are allowed to fetch, and they build a card from the
# Open Graph tags rather than from these directives. If a card ever stops appearing, check
# whether the platform started honouring `nosnippet` before rewriting the tags.
#
# `noarchive` suppresses cached copies; `nosnippet` suppresses the excerpt. Ratings here
# are anonymous opinions about named real people, so a stale cached copy outlives any
# removal request.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet
